1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,420
So what if every piece of good news showed
you who wants you to fail?

2
00:00:04,267 --> 00:00:08,067
Six weeks before the big exam. A card that
shows you who's really racing you.

3
00:00:08,087 --> 00:00:11,967
Get caught reading it, and a hundred
chairs turn their backs on you.

4
00:00:12,767 --> 00:00:14,227
It started this morning. With a stranger.

5
00:00:20,067 --> 00:00:23,527
Old woman: Oh, congratulations! Your
family must be so proud.

6
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:32,460
That's your scholarship letter. You made
the cut.

7
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:36,300
And a card just opened over a stranger,
with a number only you can see.

8
00:00:37,233 --> 00:00:41,173
You tell nobody all day. Because your best
friend hears everything first.

9
00:00:41,193 --> 00:00:42,193
He always has.

10
00:00:43,067 --> 00:00:45,867
Ten at night. Extra classes finally let
out.

11
00:00:45,887 --> 00:00:49,747
And waiting at the bottom of the steps,
like every night: your best friend.

12
00:00:54,233 --> 00:00:56,873
Because that's your best friend. And
that's minus eighty-four.

13
00:00:58,033 --> 00:00:59,493
Min-jun: Knew it. Knew it.

14
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:05,260
Sixty seconds. The card folds shut. His
arm stays. So does the number.

15
00:01:06,233 --> 00:01:06,573
The rule, once.

16
00:01:06,593 --> 00:01:11,273
Say your good news out loud, to someone's
face, and a card opens over their head.

17
00:01:11,293 --> 00:01:14,533
For sixty seconds, it shows how your news
lands with them.

18
00:01:15,367 --> 00:01:17,287
Plus one hundred means pure joy for you.

19
00:01:17,307 --> 00:01:20,027
Minus one hundred means they want you to
fail.

20
00:01:21,100 --> 00:01:22,320
And it never tells you why.

21
00:01:23,267 --> 00:01:24,267
Min-jun: You want half?

22
00:01:24,667 --> 00:01:28,447
Every night, one chocolate bar, two
halves. You take yours.

23
00:01:29,027 --> 00:01:30,627
Tonight you watch him chew.

24
00:01:31,533 --> 00:01:33,493
The only other face in the shop: the
clerk.

25
00:01:34,933 --> 00:01:35,933
Clerk: Receipt?

26
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:41,520
Zero. Grey. One click. The biggest news of
your life, and you're a receipt.

27
00:01:42,533 --> 00:01:43,713
So you try the obvious cheat.

28
00:01:43,733 --> 00:01:47,633
Make something up, and scan the whole
world for free.

29
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:56,320
Nothing. A lie opens nothing. The card
works on good news that's true, and yours.

30
00:01:56,980 --> 00:01:58,780
So you've got exactly one letter,

31
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:01,000
and you're spending it on a man who scans
snacks.

32
00:02:02,100 --> 00:02:05,800
The cat on the crate. Nothing. Not even a
card.

33
00:02:05,820 --> 00:02:10,120
So either a cat isn't a face, or a cat
doesn't care. You'll never know.

34
00:02:10,933 --> 00:02:12,413
Your phone. Your sister.

35
00:02:12,433 --> 00:02:16,253
Six years of night shifts at a warehouse
to pay for that classroom. You tell her.

36
00:02:17,033 --> 00:02:18,033
Ji-yoon: Say it again.

37
00:02:18,867 --> 00:02:21,707
You do. You even switch on video, so you
can see her grin.

38
00:02:21,727 --> 00:02:23,967
And nothing opens over a screen.

39
00:02:24,900 --> 00:02:30,960
So. Only out loud. Only to a face you can
see. Remember that. It's going to matter.

40
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:36,400
Next morning. You've worked it out. Red is
for enemies. So you go looking for one.

41
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:41,180
The rival. Trash-talks you every morning,
two years running.

42
00:02:42,133 --> 00:02:43,133
Tae-ho: Still here?

43
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:45,100
You hold up the letter.

44
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:48,240
Minus six. He barely looks up.

45
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:50,180
Tae-ho: Nice. Move.

46
00:02:51,233 --> 00:02:54,633
Two years of trash talk: minus six. You
wanted an enemy.

47
00:02:55,053 --> 00:02:56,093
You got a receipt with legs.

48
00:02:57,100 --> 00:02:58,100
Your maths teacher.

49
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:00,300
Teacher: Good.

50
00:03:00,533 --> 00:03:02,453
One word. And plus forty-one.

51
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:08,600
The mirror in the school toilets. Nothing
opens. So a mirror isn't a face.

52
00:03:08,620 --> 00:03:10,300
Or the one card you can't see is yours.

53
00:03:11,267 --> 00:03:15,547
Outside the station, a tour group. Eleven
of them, following a flag.

54
00:03:15,567 --> 00:03:16,667
You say it to all eleven.

55
00:03:17,933 --> 00:03:21,333
Eleven cards. Eleven zeros, in a row, like
a timetable.

56
00:03:22,433 --> 00:03:26,473
The bus home. A little kid in a cap, feet
swinging. You try him.

57
00:03:27,267 --> 00:03:28,267
Kid: What did you win?

58
00:03:29,533 --> 00:03:30,973
Plus seventy-seven.

59
00:03:30,993 --> 00:03:34,013
The highest number of the day is a six-
year-old who doesn't know what a

60
00:03:34,033 --> 00:03:35,033
scholarship is.

61
00:03:35,767 --> 00:03:38,407
So a kid gives you everything, your enemy
gives you nothing,

62
00:03:38,427 --> 00:03:41,167
and your best friend gives you minus
eighty-four. Why?

63
00:03:42,167 --> 00:03:46,527
We measure ourselves against people near
our own level, not far above or below.

64
00:03:47,533 --> 00:03:51,213
And envy picks someone who started where
you did, in a thing that matters to you.

65
00:03:52,067 --> 00:03:55,067
The kid isn't in the race. The rival is
twenty places ahead.

66
00:03:55,087 --> 00:03:59,347
Your best friend has the next desk, the
same chocolate bar, the same exam.

67
00:04:00,067 --> 00:04:01,067
But here's the problem.

68
00:04:01,527 --> 00:04:05,607
Problem one: the only people who can bleed
at your news are the ones in your lane.

69
00:04:05,627 --> 00:04:09,847
Which means the card is useless on
strangers, and lethal on friends.

70
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:15,020
So why not just ask him? You do, on the
academy steps.

71
00:04:19,433 --> 00:04:20,433
Min-jun: What number?

72
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:22,300
Min-jun: Is it a bug? Get it off.

73
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:25,180
He can't see it. Nobody sees the cards but
you.

74
00:04:25,200 --> 00:04:29,480
And people don't like to admit being
maliciously envious, even to themselves.

75
00:04:30,733 --> 00:04:33,853
So asking is out. You'll have to read
them. All of them.

76
00:04:34,633 --> 00:04:38,633
Eleven. Home. The letter's already on the
fridge,

77
00:04:38,653 --> 00:04:41,033
next to the jar where her paintbrushes
have stood for six years.

78
00:04:41,053 --> 00:04:45,253
She's in the doorway, pulling on her vest.
She doesn't turn round.

79
00:04:46,167 --> 00:04:47,167
Ji-yoon: Say it again.

80
00:04:50,633 --> 00:04:53,733
You say it to her back. Nothing opens. A
back is not a face.

81
00:04:53,753 --> 00:04:56,953
Your sister has the only unreadable face
in Seoul,

82
00:04:56,973 --> 00:04:58,933
because she never points it at you.

83
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:01,580
The national exam grades on a curve.

84
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:04,960
In most subjects, only the top 4 per cent
get the top grade.

85
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:10,600
So you picture your class. A hundred of
you. Four places. You're fourth.

86
00:05:11,060 --> 00:05:12,060
He's fifth.

87
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:13,860
And here's the gift inside the lie rule.

88
00:05:13,880 --> 00:05:18,300
A mock score is true, and there's a new
one every week. Fresh ammunition.

89
00:05:19,433 --> 00:05:23,353
Two weeks. Each score, you stand up, say
it, and read the room.

90
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:25,200
Teacher: Sit down.

91
00:05:26,100 --> 00:05:31,580
Anyone who goes red: out of the group
chat. Him first. No past papers. No notes.

92
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:35,240
Week one, three red. Week two, nine.

93
00:05:36,200 --> 00:05:41,720
But people notice. You brag, and you stare
at the air above their heads.

94
00:05:41,740 --> 00:05:43,460
The group chat has a name for you now.

95
00:05:44,367 --> 00:05:46,227
Tae-ho: Weatherman. Cloudy today?

96
00:05:47,267 --> 00:05:48,327
And every night at the counter:

97
00:05:49,367 --> 00:05:50,367
Min-jun: You want half?

98
00:05:50,767 --> 00:05:54,207
You take the smaller half. On principle.
And his number keeps going down.

99
00:05:55,533 --> 00:05:58,553
Then your maths teacher does the worst
thing a teacher can do.

100
00:05:59,467 --> 00:06:00,467
Teacher: Up.

101
00:06:00,600 --> 00:06:04,140
The school assembly. Five hundred faces.
She wants you to announce it.

102
00:06:07,033 --> 00:06:12,453
Five hundred cards. Grey in rows, like
seats. One red row: yours.

103
00:06:12,473 --> 00:06:14,773
And one blazing teal at the very back: the
janitor,

104
00:06:14,793 --> 00:06:17,153
who thinks you said the heating's fixed.

105
00:06:18,067 --> 00:06:21,927
Just your class, or all of Seoul? Why not
go on the news and scan the whole country?

106
00:06:21,947 --> 00:06:27,527
Because a screen isn't a face. Your
sister's video call settled that.

107
00:06:27,547 --> 00:06:30,207
But a crowd is a thousand faces, and this
street has one.

108
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:34,880
This one neighbourhood in Seoul has about
1,000 after-school academies.

109
00:06:35,800 --> 00:06:37,800
And Seoul lets them teach only until ten
at night.

110
00:06:37,820 --> 00:06:40,900
Inspectors have patrolled the streets to
make sure.

111
00:06:41,900 --> 00:06:45,840
So at ten, every door on the street opens
at once. A thousand faces.

112
00:06:45,860 --> 00:06:49,580
You climb the academy steps and say it
into the crowd.

113
00:06:53,133 --> 00:06:55,213
You just told a thousand strangers about
your scholarship.

114
00:06:55,233 --> 00:06:59,213
Nine hundred and eighty-eight of them
would like you to get off the steps.

115
00:07:00,133 --> 00:07:02,313
And every red card points back to your
class.

116
00:07:03,300 --> 00:07:06,560
The girl who borrows your calculator.
Minus thirty.

117
00:07:06,580 --> 00:07:10,300
The boy you walked home with all of first
year. Minus forty-four.

118
00:07:11,333 --> 00:07:14,633
So. Red means they want you to fail. And
your list was right.

119
00:07:15,533 --> 00:07:18,533
You climb. Fourth. Third. Second.

120
00:07:18,553 --> 00:07:21,333
And the morning you hit second, the rival
finally looks up.

121
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:23,100
Tae-ho: Weather?

122
00:07:23,867 --> 00:07:27,467
Minus seventy-one. Two years of trash talk
gave you minus six.

123
00:07:27,487 --> 00:07:31,367
One mock score gave you minus seventy-one.
You're in his lane now.

124
00:07:31,387 --> 00:07:35,987
Your rival finally hates you. The enemy
you went looking for has finally arrived.

125
00:07:37,300 --> 00:07:39,960
And your best friend? Minus ninety-one.

126
00:07:39,980 --> 00:07:43,300
Second place, and the reddest card in the
room is at the next desk.

127
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:45,140
So what does first cost?

128
00:07:46,133 --> 00:07:50,633
The late-night study room. Past midnight.
His seat's empty, and his notebook's open.

129
00:07:50,653 --> 00:07:53,033
You look. Obviously.

130
00:07:56,733 --> 00:08:01,093
Your mistakes. Every one. Copied out, with
a "why?", and the fix.

131
00:08:01,933 --> 00:08:05,693
He isn't sawing your chair. He's building
a ladder. Out of your mistakes.

132
00:08:06,413 --> 00:08:08,113
You've been his textbook for two years.

133
00:08:09,033 --> 00:08:11,793
Envy comes in two kinds: one pushes you to
move up,

134
00:08:11,813 --> 00:08:14,993
the other pushes you to pull the other
person down.

135
00:08:15,867 --> 00:08:19,387
Minus never proved they wanted you to
fail. It only proved your win hurt.

136
00:08:19,407 --> 00:08:23,907
In one brain-scan study, envying a peer
who did better at something that mattered

137
00:08:23,927 --> 00:08:26,507
came with activity in a region also active
in pain.

138
00:08:27,433 --> 00:08:29,253
A card has one colour for every kind of
hurt.

139
00:08:29,273 --> 00:08:33,053
Which is sweet, until you remember the
rival is minus seventy-one,

140
00:08:33,653 --> 00:08:34,993
and he does not build ladders.

141
00:08:35,967 --> 00:08:39,687
But here's problem two. Every time, you
read your best friend first.

142
00:08:39,707 --> 00:08:41,347
And the rival has been watching.

143
00:08:42,300 --> 00:08:43,720
Tae-ho: You only brag to faces.

144
00:08:44,967 --> 00:08:48,187
He doesn't know about the cards. He
doesn't need to.

145
00:08:48,207 --> 00:08:50,827
He's worked out the shape of it: you say
your news to a face,

146
00:08:50,847 --> 00:08:53,887
and you read something. So he gives the
class one word.

147
00:08:54,733 --> 00:08:55,733
Tae-ho: Backs.

148
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:04,380
Monday. You stand up with your score. A
hundred chairs scrape. A hundred backs.

149
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:07,000
You wanted to avoid the red ones.

150
00:09:07,160 --> 00:09:10,720
Now the whole room is avoiding you, and
they don't need to see your face to do it.

151
00:09:11,433 --> 00:09:16,473
No faces. No cards. Your edge is gone,
your phone says "removed from group",

152
00:09:16,493 --> 00:09:17,813
and the exam is in three weeks.

153
00:09:18,667 --> 00:09:21,447
The night before, you leave your own
notebook on his desk.

154
00:09:21,987 --> 00:09:25,847
Every mistake you made this year, and the
fix. Half a chocolate bar on top.

155
00:09:25,867 --> 00:09:31,747
The bigger half. You're competitive, not a
monster. It might cost you first place.

156
00:09:31,767 --> 00:09:35,187
You leave it anyway. If he's building a
ladder, he can have the good wood.

157
00:09:36,067 --> 00:09:37,067
Exam morning.

158
00:09:37,287 --> 00:09:41,427
Outside every exam school, parents crowd
the gate to see their kids in.

159
00:09:41,447 --> 00:09:44,027
And there in the crowd, straight off the
night shift,

160
00:09:44,047 --> 00:09:45,427
still in her vest: your sister.

161
00:09:46,087 --> 00:09:48,547
With a lunch box packed like the exam
lasts a week.

162
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:55,960
Neither of you says a word. Nothing opens.
Some faces you don't read.

163
00:09:56,833 --> 00:09:59,813
The gate closes behind you. And the whole
country goes quiet.

164
00:10:00,767 --> 00:10:04,587
It's true. In November 2025, during the
English listening test,

165
00:10:04,967 --> 00:10:08,067
South Korea stopped its planes taking off
and landing for 35 minutes,

166
00:10:08,087 --> 00:10:10,587
so their noise wouldn't disturb the
students.

167
00:10:14,167 --> 00:10:17,447
A country holds its breath for you.
Results come in a month.

168
00:10:17,947 --> 00:10:19,707
For a month, you say nothing to anyone.

169
00:10:20,600 --> 00:10:24,120
Results. Top grade. Top grade. Top grade.
The scholarship holds.

170
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:26,980
And here's what you had backwards.

171
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:31,620
You treated the four per cent like four
places in a room. But in most subjects,

172
00:10:31,640 --> 00:10:35,000
it's the top 4 per cent of everyone in the
country who sat it.

173
00:10:35,020 --> 00:10:37,480
Four seats, a hundred backs, six weeks of
war.

174
00:10:37,500 --> 00:10:38,800
For a seat that was never in the room.

175
00:10:39,633 --> 00:10:44,453
Your sister's asleep on the sofa after the
shift. You nudge her. One eye opens.

176
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,920
Plus one hundred. Before the other eye
even opens.

177
00:10:49,967 --> 00:10:53,087
The academy, that night. Everyone has a
result.

178
00:10:53,107 --> 00:10:54,787
The rival stands up before you can.

179
00:10:55,633 --> 00:10:59,093
Tae-ho: Backs. Go on, weatherman. Say it
again.

180
00:11:00,133 --> 00:11:03,253
A hundred backs. Facing the windows.

181
00:11:03,273 --> 00:11:06,353
And it's dark outside, so every window is
a mirror.

182
00:11:07,133 --> 00:11:09,993
A hundred faces in the glass, all looking
your way.

183
00:11:10,867 --> 00:11:13,487
The card never said a face had to be the
right way round.

184
00:11:20,967 --> 00:11:23,967
A face in a mirror is still a face. The
toilets weren't broken.

185
00:11:23,987 --> 00:11:26,727
The one card you can't see was always
yours.

186
00:11:27,767 --> 00:11:30,767
And the room, in the glass: nearly all
teal.

187
00:11:30,787 --> 00:11:34,287
Most of them were only ever red at the
race, and the race is over.

188
00:11:35,167 --> 00:11:40,407
Your best friend? Minus eighty-four.
Still. He's glad for you.

189
00:11:40,427 --> 00:11:42,767
He also wants to beat you at everything
until you both die.

190
00:11:42,787 --> 00:11:44,607
That's your best friend.

191
00:11:45,967 --> 00:11:47,107
And the rival is back to minus six.

192
00:11:47,127 --> 00:11:50,687
Between rivals, that's practically a
handshake.

193
00:11:51,533 --> 00:11:52,533
Tae-ho: Weather?

194
00:11:53,300 --> 00:11:57,680
Morning. The fridge. Your sister, the jar
of brushes, and the door.

195
00:11:57,700 --> 00:12:02,480
Your best friend. A letter of his own. For
the first time, the good news isn't yours.

196
00:12:03,567 --> 00:12:04,567
Min-jun: I got in!

197
00:12:08,833 --> 00:12:09,833
Ji-yoon: Say it again.

198
00:12:12,467 --> 00:12:14,847
Now it's his news. Your turn to be the
friend.

199
00:12:14,867 --> 00:12:18,987
Be honest: what number would open over
your head? Tell us in the comments.

200
00:12:19,007 --> 00:12:22,227
And if you'd have done the same, hit like
and subscribe.
